<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
  <overview>
    <p>
      A GitHub release URL containing <code>/releases/latest/download/</code> can resolve to
      different artifacts over time. An Azure DevOps pipeline that downloads from this URL
      can consume a different release without a corresponding change to the pipeline.
    </p>
    <p>
      This query identifies literal floating release URLs in download command lines within
      inline Azure DevOps script steps and inline PowerShell, Bash, and CmdLine task inputs.
      It recognizes curl, wget, Invoke-WebRequest, Invoke-RestMethod, Start-BitsTransfer,
      and their listed aliases or executable names.
      Detection is text-based, not an analysis of shell execution or artifact data flow.
      URLs assembled through variables, external scripts, and other task types are not
      covered. A finding does not establish that the artifact is executed or lacks
      integrity verification. A separate hash or signature check does not suppress a
      floating-reference finding.
    </p>
  </overview>
  <recommendation>
    <p>
      Replace the floating URL with an explicitly selected version. Version selection alone
      does not establish integrity: a versioned release asset or tag can still be changed.
      Verify the downloaded artifact against a trusted expected SHA-256 or stronger hash,
      or an appropriate trusted signature, and fail before consuming the artifact if
      verification fails. Where required by the organization's build policy, use internally
      validated and hosted artifacts instead of direct external downloads.
    </p>
  </recommendation>
  <example>
    <p>
      This pipeline downloads whichever release is currently marked as latest:
    </p>
    <pre><![CDATA[
steps:
  - bash: |
      curl -fL https://github.com/example/tool/releases/latest/download/tool.tar.gz -o tool.tar.gz
]]></pre>
    <p>
      An explicit release URL such as
      <code>https://github.com/example/tool/releases/download/v1.2.3/tool.tar.gz</code>
      avoids floating version selection, but still requires integrity verification before use.
    </p>
  </example>
  <references>
    <li>
      GitHub Docs:
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/releasing-projects-on-github/linking-to-releases">Linking to releases</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Common Weakness Enumeration:
      <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/829.html">CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere</a>.
    </li>
  </references>
</qhelp>
